{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('METHOD/LIMITATION GROUNDING')\nfor index,claim in enumerate(claims):\n    if not claim['citation_keys'] and index>=31:\n        print(index,claim['claim'])\nstrategy=json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\nfor key in ['central_question','central_thesis','edit_framing_decision','notes_for_writer']:\n print('\\\\nSTRATEGY',key, json.dumps(strategy[key],ensure_ascii=False))\ndata=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nprint('\\\\nPAPER DATA totals',data['totals'])\nprint('Grounding source IDs',list(data['grounding_sources']))\nprint('Final evaluation fields',list(data['final_evaluation']))\nfor key,value in data['final_evaluation'].items():\n if key not in ['evaluation_evidence']:\n  print(key, str(value)[:800])\nprint('Evidence keys',list(data['final_evaluation']['evaluation_evidence']))\nprint('Episodes type',type(data['final_evaluation']['evaluation_evidence']['episodes']).__name__)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"METHOD/LIMITATION GROUNDING\n31 Observed-assignment fitted residual RMSE spans [0.9762526322574112, 1.0808042947319605], near unit noise, while threshold and two-hop endpoint errors are substantial; this is descriptive, not causal.\n32 SBM Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0, \"linear_design_condition\": 19.02354932593077, \"maximum\": 0.7693330536163302, \"minimum\": 0.23716191363617892}\n33 SBM Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.000518798828125, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0006103515625, \"linear_design_condition\": 13.26309780585197, \"maximum\": 0.9822164144943205, \"minimum\": 0.01698245843747851}\n34 BA Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.013427734375, \"fraction_zero\": 0.012715657552083334, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.588901196448447, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n35 BA Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.032928466796875, \"fraction_zero\": 0.027394612630208332, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.7038367703447435, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n36 WS Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.0015157063802083333, \"fraction_zero\": 0.0014241536458333333, \"linear_design_condition\": 8.807364526266976, \"maximum\": 0.9965277777777777, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n37 WS Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.09168497721354167, \"fraction_zero\": 0.10963948567708333, \"linear_design_condition\": 6.682392671890018, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n38 Stanford3 Strong linear seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03205736808792221, \"fraction_zero\": 0.03242059382012774, \"linear_design_condition\": 9.193799507457536, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n39 Stanford3 Cluster seed-mean exposure support: {\"fraction_one\": 0.03979299729558663, \"fraction_zero\": 0.04890960354450774, \"linear_design_condition\": 7.208791238224571, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"minimum\": 0.0}\n40 Ratio plots divide cell RMSEs and bootstrap complete seed blocks. The resampling uses 10000 draws, seed 20261107, and 95% percentile intervals. Intervals quantify fixed-benchmark Monte Carlo variation, not causal coverage or graph-population generalization.\n41 The benchmark uses 4096-node synthetic SBM, BA and WS graphs, and fixed Stanford3 with 11586 nodes and 568309 undirected edges. SBM uses four blocks with probabilities .04/.002; BA attachment is five; WS neighborhood is ten with rewiring .15. Covariates have one column. Synthetic topologies vary by seed and Stanford3 topology does not.\n42 The outcome model is .5*x + z + spillover + unit Gaussian noise. Six spillovers are 0, .25*e, 2*e, 2*I(e>=.75)+z*e, 2*h, and 2*e under cluster assignment. Recorded GATEs are respectively 1, 1.25, 3, 4, 3, 3. Two-hop exposure includes backtracking; isolates have zero exposure.\n43 Individual assignment is Bernoulli(.5). The cluster design uses 32 deterministic balanced graph-BFS clusters and independent Bernoulli(.5) cluster assignment; it changes the joint assignment distribution rather than exposure support alone.\n44 The inspected associated estimator has quadratic one-hop features, saved observed-data standardization, SVD, endpoint-contrast risk grid n*{0,1e-6,1e-4,1e-2,1}, and deterministic minimum selection. Source association is not an independently reconstructed executable-identity proof.\n46 Rank tolerance is max(design shape)*machine epsilon*smax, contrast residual tolerance 1e-8*max(1,norm(q)), residual df n-r-1. The fallback uses hinges .25,.5,.75, ridge 1e-3, and unpenalized intercept. Exact-constant outcomes have zero contrast. Fallback and penalty frequencies are unavailable.\n47 The three substantive comparators are difference in means, covariate-adjusted own-treatment OLS and linear one-hop OLS, evaluated on matched cases. OLS endpoint contrast accounts for nonisolated-node fraction.\n48 Component ablations, same-reference GCV comparison, original-hinge matched final comparison, inference coverage, risk calibration and additional noise studies are absent; missing measurements do not establish inferior performance.\n49 Recorded control success is 1 and failed-episode fraction 0. Qualification and limited behavioral controls do not establish arbitrary-interference validity; candidate internal diagnostics are unverified.\n50 The evaluator caps signed errors at magnitude 10 and normalizes by unit noise; it averages cell RMSEs and absolute biases equally, not pooled squared errors.\n51 Reproduction uses archived PCG64-generated inputs, seed+100003*(family ordinal+1), the listed 24 seeds, declared NumPy1.26.4/SciPy1.13.1/NetworkX3.3, two CPU cores and 8GiB per role, 7200s wall allowance. Recorded elapsed evaluation is approximately629s, not a per-method runtime.\n\nSTRATEGY central_question \"How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\"\n\nSTRATEGY central_thesis \"In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\"\n\nSTRATEGY edit_framing_decision \"This is a fresh framing, not a manuscript-edit run: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or contributions scaffold is present. Recentered denotes moving from the source task's broad accuracy agenda to the supported endpoint-regression comparison and its limitations; it does not assert that a prior manuscript was revised. Preserving a hypothetical prior story would invent evidence. Broadening to a shared estimator/architecture family is unsupported. Centering the completed final comparison is preferable to either broadening or a development-improvement narrative.\"\n\nSTRATEGY notes_for_writer [\"Include the venue-required separate LLM Usage section after references, alongside the five evidence-backed scientific supplement subsections. Disclose documented LLM roles in ideation, candidate-code generation, reference-query planning, framing, writing, and figure preparation as applicable. Use actual process artifacts under results/ideas and the local strategy/agent records; distinguish work already performed from future writing/figure activity. These process artifacts, not scientific metric source IDs, ground the disclosure, so it is a separate process-disclosure requirement rather than an entry in supplementary_plan.planned_subsections. Include no author identities, personal links, funding, or acknowledgements.\", \"Follow write-paper-standalone only at the later drafting stage. This strategy is ready for a bounded comparison paper, not a claim of publication acceptance, novel causal identification, or final-deliverable completeness. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, or run final paper validation during strategy generation.\", \"ICLR 2026 is fixed by VENUE.md: anonymized conference submission, 8-9 body pages excluding references; an eight-page body must finish at least 75% full. No names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying repositories, or internal URLs. Include a separate LLM Usage section, preferably after references. The supplied paper.sty confirms single-column 5.5in by 9in geometry; its historical header does not independently verify official 2026 template provenance. Check official-template provenance during final preparation and regenerate figures if geometry differs, rather than silently treating this as a double-column format.\", \"Target approximately 8.75 substantive body pages including figures and tables: title/abstract 0.40; introduction and early overview 1.10; related work 0.65; target and interference setup 0.90; endpoint-tuned estimator 1.50; empirical protocol and condition table 0.95; conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures 2.15; discussion/limitations 0.85; conclusion 0.25. These are layout allocations, not measured page counts. Compress repetition rather than exceeding nine pages; spend spare space explaining the risk formula, baseline definitions, aggregation, uncertainty, and failure cases, never diary or filler.\", \"Use thesis-first headings such as Global Intervention Target, Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression, Empirical Protocol, Conditional Accuracy and Failure, and Scope and Limitations. Present all networks as condition-specific evidence under one question. The introduction's contributions are an implemented target-aware regression construction, a matched conditional comparison, and an explicit accuracy/fit limitation map; do not claim isolated benefits of curvature features or risk tuning.\", \"Define GATE as the finite-population average difference between all-treated and all-control potential outcomes. The fitted estimate averages node-aligned predictions at those two assignments. Define neighbor exposure with isolates assigned zero; all-treated exposure need not equal one for isolates. Describe two-hop interference as length-two random-walk exposure including backtracking, not unique-distance-two exposure or the failed branch's nonbacktracking features.\", \"Map condition IDs to scientific names using the final manifest: no spillover; weak linear neighbor spillover; strong linear neighbor spillover; threshold-plus-treatment interaction; two-hop spillover; and strong linear spillover under deterministic graph-BFS cluster assignment. The last condition changes assignment, not the outcome mechanism relative to strong linear spillover. All outcomes are synthetic, including on Stanford3; no real platform outcome or social causal effect is measured.\", \"Explain the implemented endpoint-risk score as (a_lambda^T y_centered - a_0^T y_centered)^2 + 2*sigma_hat^2*a_lambda^T*a_0 - sigma_hat^2*||a_0||^2, where a_0 represents the estimable reference contrast and a_lambda the fixed-penalty contrast. State the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions, the row-space and degrees-of-freedom guards, and the lack of an unbiased post-selection interpretation. These are implementation/conditional analytical facts, not evidence that the assumptions hold in every condition or a new established theorem.\", \"No same-feature fixed-penalty, OLS, or predictive-GCV ablations have usable final evidence. The proposed estimator jointly changes feature representation and regularization relative to the original fixed-hinge regression; comparisons with simple one-hop OLS likewise change more than one component. Do not attribute any improvement or regression solely to tuning, curvature, support, conditioning, or the fallback. Do not turn algebraic estimability into positivity, identification, or generalization.\", \"Uncertainty must be recomputed from the 24 existing seed-level episode records, preserving every graph/condition/method observation within a resampled seed block. For ratios, recompute both methods' RMSEs in each resample before dividing; for the table, resample blocks before equal-cell averaging. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap with a fixed documented random seed may be used; specify resample count when implemented. No intervals have yet been produced. These quantify Monte Carlo variation in this fixed benchmark, not unit-level causal confidence-interval coverage or independent graph-population generalization. Per-condition comparisons are descriptive/exploratory and multiplicity is not resolved by the bootstrap.\", \"Ground every scientific result view in final.evidence.episodes and the appropriate final.metric.* fields. The final qualification facts are completed, qualified, and true under final.qualification.status/outcome/correct, independently of downstream study metadata. Gate diagnostics not provided remain unknown; infer no validity/adaptivity failure or guarantee. If authoritative later evidence reports not_qualified, retain that outcome and its stated limitations in writing and review; do not infer its cause from retained metrics or erase it on publication grounds.\", \"PRIVATE-ONLY provenance: paper_data.best_correct, result.best_correct_score, and results/best_program_manifest.json locate the available selected source; the final manifest supplies the frozen program-tree identifier. downstream_study and study.* are absent. Do not invent study.* IDs, expose selection-score internals in visible prose, report scored/unscored attempt counts, or subtract search and final scores. Do not silently treat the development-only original fixed-hinge method as a recorded final baseline. Missing two-hop performance does not justify eliminating two-hop adjustment as a scientific possibility.\", \"Keep copy-observed and constant-prediction checks out of the substantive four-method comparison; they are degenerate behavioral controls, not credible scientific alternatives. Report task integrity checks briefly and do not let a high composite utility stand in for effect-estimation accuracy. Do not invent inference coverage, runtimes per estimator, selected-penalty histograms, contrast-weight norms, support probabilities, or fallback rates from fields that do not exist.\", \"Required figure budgets are explicit: overview one panel; accuracy four panels arranged two per row; diagnostics two panels in one row. Never exceed three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel; the tighter figure-specific limits govern. All figures target the single full text column. Pair the quantitative comparison with a compact main table of absolute RMSE and absolute bias for all four methods, and a protocol table defining conditions and graph sizes. Do not substitute a favorable-cell ranking for the complete condition matrix.\", \"Vector-first output: quantitative plotting scripts must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG from the same source; LaTeX must use the PDF whenever it exists. The overview uses generate-concept-figure exactly once and preserves editable draw.io source; request a vector PDF export if available, otherwise regenerate raster output at least 2400px wide for this full-width placement. Any column-width raster in a changed layout must be at least 1200px wide. Never upscale existing rasters. If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, log the concrete reason before any documented vector fallback; do not silently replace it with an improvised schematic.\", \"References are not an experiment. references.bib currently contains permitted starter-title matches Chin2018RegressionAF and Han2023ModelbasedRA, but references_provenance.json records retrieval metadata, not verified reading, and other returned statistical titles are not automatically relevant foundations for this criterion. Cite only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources. Do not pad related work with unrelated retrieved hits, rely on questionable metadata years without checking, or invent missing graph-attribution citation keys. Any additional acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step, not to claims grounded in this strategy.\", \"Respect the restricted-literature boundary: do not identify the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read related-paper/reproduction/discussion pages. Allowed starter-source checks and graph provenance must not become unrestricted searches. If restricted material is encountered, stop and record where and how much was seen. No external research or new scientific runs were performed during this framing stage.\", \"The supplement is a minimum grounded scope, not an authoritative posthoc ranking. Route later appendix-ranked analyses there without retroactively changing this strategy, and keep evidence essential to the central thesis in the main paper. Proposed missing studies, such as matched tuning ablations, noise stress tests, hidden endpoint twins, and inference calibration, belong in limitations/future work and must never be written as completed results.\"]\n\nPAPER DATA totals {'attempts': 3, 'correct_programs': 2, 'evaluated_programs': 2, 'ideas': 3}\nGrounding source IDs ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.combined_score', 'final.metric.control_success_fraction', 'final.metric.failed_episode_fraction', 'final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias', 'final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'result.best_any_score', 'result.best_correct_score', 'result.improvement_001', 'result.improvement_002', 'result.top_program_01', 'result.top_program_02', 'result.total_attempts', 'result.total_correct_programs', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_ideas']\nFinal evaluation fields ['correct', 'evaluation_evidence', 'evaluator_sha256', 'manifest_path', 'metrics', 'outcome', 'program_tree_sha256', 'results_tree_sha256', 'status', 'task_tree_sha256']\ncorrect True\nevaluator_sha256 sha256:273cf370e3c2aaeb50a4659fbb1c8cff99d1d042bc6afa02f90594cef531e5e3\nmanifest_path work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\nmetrics {'combined_score': 0.9931177679506574, 'control_success_fraction': 1.0, 'failed_episode_fraction': 0.0, 'mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias': 0.4597691498768297, 'mean_capped_normalized_rmse': 0.6140475146408374}\noutcome qualified\nprogram_tree_sha256 sha256:37121e22c23552d3c9168e22661f12879935b238b6838ce94dc038a7eaa15e72\nresults_tree_sha256 sha256:3c3bcb43740a1d90e9761ad07a965974af2ceb71ec65e586784027ba8e9f049a\nstatus completed\ntask_tree_sha256 sha256:2697fa6dc4a81915db3cf9aaa0a6298cb5df2b6a99acb60e47c825617266184f\nEvidence keys ['candidate_diagnostics_trust', 'episode_count', 'episodes', 'path', 'sha256', 'status']\nEpisodes type list\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
