{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor reference in provenance['references']:\n print(reference['citation_key']+' | '+reference['title']+' | '+reference['reading_scope'])\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('\\\\nCLAIM 45',json.dumps(claims[45],ensure_ascii=False))\nstrategy=json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\nfor key in ['central_thesis','edit_framing_decision']:\n print(key+': '+strategy[key])\nPY\nfor p in reference_evidence/Chin2019.txt reference_evidence/Han2023.txt; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$p\"; cat \"$p\"; done\nprintf '\"'\\\\n--- MEMORY TAIL ---\\\\n'; tail -12 memory.md\nprintf '\\\\n--- CONCLUSIONS ---\\\\n'; if [ -f global_memory/conclusions.md ]; then cat global_memory/conclusions.md; fi\nprintf '\\\\n--- HELPER AND GLOBAL LOGS ---\\\\n'; ls .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py global_memory/memory.md global_memory/memory.jsonl\nsha256sum paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json > /tmp/draft_gap_protected_files.sha256\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor reference in provenance['references']:\n print(reference['citation_key']+' | '+reference['title']+' | '+reference['reading_scope'])\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nprint('\\\\nCLAIM 45',json.dumps(claims[45],ensure_ascii=False))\nstrategy=json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())\nfor key in ['central_thesis','edit_framing_decision']:\n print(key+': '+strategy[key])\nPY\nfor p in reference_evidence/Chin2019.txt reference_evidence/Han2023.txt; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$p\"; cat \"$p\"; done\nprintf '\"'\\\\n--- MEMORY TAIL ---\\\\n'; tail -12 memory.md\nprintf '\\\\n--- CONCLUSIONS ---\\\\n'; if [ -f global_memory/conclusions.md ]; then cat global_memory/conclusions.md; fi\nprintf '\\\\n--- HELPER AND GLOBAL LOGS ---\\\\n'; ls .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py global_memory/memory.md global_memory/memory.jsonl\nsha256sum paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json > /tmp/draft_gap_protected_files.sha256\"","aggregated_output":"Aronow2017 | Estimating average causal effects under general interference, with application to a social network experiment | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nUgander2013 | Graph cluster randomization: network exposure to multiple universes | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nEckles2017 | Design and Analysis of Experiments in Networks: Reducing Bias from Interference | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nChin2019 | Regression Adjustments for Estimating the Global Treatment Effect in Experiments with Interference | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nHan2023 | Model-based regression adjustment with model-free covariates for network interference | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nKarrer2021 | Network Experimentation at Scale | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nCortez2022 | Staggered Rollout Designs Enable Causal Inference Under Interference Without Network Knowledge | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nTraud2012 | Social structure of Facebook networks | Original document abstract and introduction; no citation graph access\nRossi2015 | The Network Data Repository with Interactive Graph Analytics and Visualization | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nChoi2012 | Stochastic blockmodels with a growing number of classes | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nBarabasi1999 | Emergence of Scaling in Random Networks | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nWatts1998 | Collective dynamics of ‘small-world’ networks | Europe PMC abstract; no citation graph or related-content access\nHoerl1970 | Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nGolub1979 | Generalized Cross-Validation as a Method for Choosing a Good Ridge Parameter | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nStein1981 | Estimation of the Mean of a Multivariate Normal Distribution | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nEfron1986 | How Biased is the Apparent Error Rate of a Prediction Rule? | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nWhite1980 | A Heteroskedasticity-Consistent Covariance Matrix Estimator and a Direct Test for Heteroskedasticity | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nEfron1979 | Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nRubin1974 | Estimating causal effects of treatments in randomized and nonrandomized studies. | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nHudgens2008 | Toward Causal Inference With Interference | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nManski2013 | Identification of treatment response with social interactions | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nLin2013 | Agnostic notes on regression adjustments to experimental data: Reexamining Freedman’s critique | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nFreedman2008 | On regression adjustments to experimental data | Original document abstract and introduction; no citation graph access\nSearle1965 | Additional Results Concerning Estimable Functions and Generalized Inverse Matrices | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\nNeyman1990 | On the Application of Probability Theory to Agricultural Experiments. Essay on Principles. Section 9 | Title and abstract only; no citation graph or related-content access\n\nCLAIM 45 {\"claim\": \"The fixed-penalty risk expression equals squared bias plus contrast variance only under the stated correct-reference and noise assumptions. The selected minimum is not an unbiased selected-risk estimate; empirical calibration is unmeasured.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Stein1981\", \"Efron1986\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/estimator.py#L127-L151\"], \"scope\": \"Recorded fixed benchmark or explicitly conditional source-based specification; no causal component attribution\"}\ncentral_thesis: In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\nedit_framing_decision: This is a fresh framing, not a manuscript-edit run: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or contributions scaffold is present. Recentered denotes moving from the source task's broad accuracy agenda to the supported endpoint-regression comparison and its limitations; it does not assert that a prior manuscript was revised. Preserving a hypothetical prior story would invent evidence. Broadening to a shared estimator/architecture family is unsupported. Centering the completed final comparison is preferable to either broadening or a development-improvement narrative.\n\n--- reference_evidence/Chin2019.txt ---\nRegression Adjustments for Estimating the Global Treatment Effect in Experiments with Interference\n\nAbstract Standard estimators of the global average treatment effect can be biased in the presence of interference. This paper proposes regression adjustment estimators for removing bias due to interference in Bernoulli randomized experiments. We use a fitted model to predict the counterfactual outcomes of global control and global treatment. Our work differs from standard regression adjustments in that the adjustment variables are constructed from functions of the treatment assignment vector, and that we allow the researcher to use a collection of any functions correlated with the response, turning the problem of detecting interference into a feature engineering problem. We characterize the distribution of the proposed estimator in a linear model setting and connect the results to the standard theory of regression adjustments under SUTVA. We then propose an estimator that allows for flexible machine learning estimators to be used for fitting a nonlinear interference functional form. We propose conducting statistical inference via bootstrap and resampling methods, which allow us to sidestep the complicated dependences implied by interference and instead rely on empirical covariance structures. Such variance estimation relies on an exogeneity assumption akin to the standard unconfoundedness assumption invoked in observational studies. In simulation experiments, our methods are better at debiasing estimates than existing inverse propensity weighted estimators based on neighborhood exposure modeling. We use our method to reanalyze an experiment concerning weather insurance adoption conducted on a collection of villages in rural China.\n\n--- reference_evidence/Han2023.txt ---\nModel-based regression adjustment with model-free covariates for network interference\n\nAbstract When estimating a global average treatment effect (GATE) under network interference, units can have widely different relationships to the treatment depending on a combination of the structure of their network neighborhood, the structure of the interference mechanism, and how the treatment was distributed in their neighborhood. In this work, we introduce a sequential procedure to generate and select graph- and treatment-based covariates for GATE estimation under regression adjustment. We show that it is possible to simultaneously achieve low bias and considerably reduce variance with such a procedure. To tackle inferential complications caused by our feature generation and selection process, we introduce a way to construct confidence intervals based on a block bootstrap. We illustrate that our selection procedure and subsequent estimator can achieve good performance in terms of root-mean-square error in several semi-synthetic experiments with Bernoulli designs, comparing favorably to an oracle estimator that takes advantage of regression adjustments for the known underlying interference structure. We apply our method to a real-world experimental dataset with strong evidence of interference and demonstrate that it can estimate the GATE reasonably well without knowing the interference process a priori .\n\n--- MEMORY TAIL ---\n### 2026-11-07T04:04:00.782915+00:00 | step=draft | scope=paper\n- summary: Drafted a standalone conditional comparison with complete cell tables, paired bootstrap summaries, source snapshots and reproducibility notes.\n- evidence: Derived mean cell RMSE .6140475 vs .4598498 one-hop OLS; paired difference .1541978 with 95% seed-block interval [.1274666,.1793079]. All 576 scientific records complete/unclipped. No new estimator runs. Replaced irrelevant bibliography hits with 25 bounded direct-source citations, checked print years and read abstracts/excerpts. Official ICLR2026 style acquired. Concept figure remains explicitly orchestrator-owned placeholder.\n- next_action: Validate all links, citations, claims, figures and rendered manuscript; retain missing-ablation and source-identity limits. Canonical compile currently rejects a system TeX configuration symlink; investigate environment without changing scientific evidence.\n- tags: gate=kept, trigger=none, reason=Reaggregation and writing add no new scientific experiment or distilled recommendation\n\n### 2026-11-07T04:12:44.703972+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper_draft_ready: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs; negative comparisons and qualification limitations are preserved.\n- evidence: Compiled paper.pdf; 77 mapped claims; 25 inspected references; complete tables; vector plots; physical archived inputs; readable source snapshots and reproducibility notes. Final qualification independently remains completed/qualified/true. Evaluator snapshot SHA matches recorded final evaluator SHA. Canonical geometric helper finds 8 body pages, .909 final-page fill, references page9 and supplement page11. Citation, claim and rendered-SI checks pass; evidence verifier has zero dangling links. Concept placeholders remain for orchestrator replacement. Strict compile wrapper rejects a system TeX config symlink between approved trees; sanitized compilation ran separately. Full-gate success and selected executable identity reconstruction are not claimed. Schema-only figure-type normalization preserves scientific framing.\n- next_action: Orchestrator should render endpoint_overview, replace placeholders, compile in its supported environment and repeat full validation. No new scientific evaluation or user input is needed.\n- tags: gate=kept, trigger=none, reason=Writing and validation organize existing evidence without new scientific experiments or recommendations\n\n\n--- CONCLUSIONS ---\n_No conclusions yet._\n\n--- HELPER AND GLOBAL LOGS ---\n.codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\nglobal_memory/memory.jsonl\nglobal_memory/memory.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
